
<% content_for :title, "Taoism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .tao-bipolar-page {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
  }

  .tao-bipolar-page *,
  .tao-bipolar-page *::before,
  .tao-bipolar-page *::after {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  /* HERO */

  .tao-bipolar-hero {
    min-height: 90vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 90px 6%;
    background-color: #080808;
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .78),
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .35) 55%,
        transparent 85%
      ),
      url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_taoism_desktop.png') %>");
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  .tao-bipolar-hero-content {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .tao-bipolar-eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 28px;
    color: #ff1493;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: .15em;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .tao-bipolar-hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 1000px;
    font-size: clamp(46px, 7vw, 105px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .94;
    letter-spacing: -.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .tao-bipolar-hero h1 span {
    display: block;
  }

  .tao-bipolar-intro {
    margin: 30px 0 0;
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: clamp(16px, 2vw, 24px);
    font-weight: 900;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* QUOTE */

  .tao-quote {
    padding: 55px 6%;
    background: #080808;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .tao-quote blockquote {
    max-width: 1000px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .tao-quote blockquote p {
    margin: 0 0 15px;
    color: #eaff00;
    font-size: clamp(28px, 4.5vw, 60px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.15;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .tao-quote cite {
    color: #fff;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* SECTIONS */

  .tao-section {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .85fr) minmax(0, 1.15fr);
    gap: 60px;
    padding: 80px max(6%, calc((100% - 1200px) / 2));
    align-items: start;
  }

  .tao-section-heading,
  .tao-copy {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .tao-number {
    margin: 0 0 20px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: .15em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .tao-section h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 4.5vw, 68px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .98;
    letter-spacing: -.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .tao-copy {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.85;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .tao-copy p {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
  }

  .tao-copy p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .tao-takeaway {
    margin-top: 28px !important;
    padding-top: 22px;
    border-top: 3px solid currentColor;
    font-weight: 900;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* COLORS */

  .tao-pink {
    background: #ff1493;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .tao-yellow {
    background: #eaff00;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .tao-black {
    background: #080808;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .tao-purple {
    background: #8a00ff;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .tao-white {
    background: #fff;
    color: #080808;
  }

  /* CLOSING */

  .tao-ending {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .85fr) minmax(0, 1.15fr);
    gap: 60px;
    padding: 85px 6%;
    background: #ff1493;
    color: #080808;
  }

  .tao-ending > div {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .tao-ending-small {
    margin: 0 0 20px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: .15em;
  }

  .tao-ending h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(48px, 7vw, 100px);
    font-weight: 900;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: .95;
    letter-spacing: -.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .tao-ending h2 span {
    color: #fff;
  }

  .tao-ending-copy {
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .tao-ending-copy p {
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }

  .tao-back-button {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 15px;
    padding: 15px 20px;
    border: 2px solid #080808;
    color: #080808;
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 900;
    letter-spacing: .08em;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    transition: .2s ease;
  }

  .tao-back-button:hover {
    background: #080808;
    color: #eaff00;
  }

  .tao-back-button:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid #eaff00;
    outline-offset: 5px;
  }

  /* MOBILE */

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .tao-bipolar-hero {
      min-height: 88svh;
      align-items: flex-start;
      padding: 50px 6% 65px;
      background-image:
        linear-gradient(
          180deg,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .55),
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .12) 65%,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .20)
        ),
        url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_taoism_mobile.png') %>");
      background-position: center, center bottom;
      background-size: cover;
    }

    .tao-bipolar-eyebrow {
      font-size: 10px;
      margin-bottom: 24px;
    }

    .tao-bipolar-hero h1 {
      font-size: clamp(40px, 9vw, 68px);
    }

    .tao-bipolar-intro {
      font-size: 14px;
      margin-top: 24px;
    }

    .tao-quote {
      padding: 45px 6%;
    }

    .tao-section {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
      padding: 55px 6%;
    }

    .tao-section h2 {
      font-size: clamp(36px, 10vw, 58px);
    }

    .tao-copy {
      font-size: 16px;
      line-height: 1.8;
    }

    .tao-ending {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
      padding: 65px 6%;
    }

    .tao-ending-copy {
      font-size: 16px;
    }
  }
</style>


<main class="tao-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="tao-bipolar-hero">

    <div class="tao-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="tao-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / TAOISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>TAOIST LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="tao-bipolar-intro">
        STOP FIGHTING THE CURRENT.<br>
        LEARN WHEN TO FLOW AND WHEN TO STOP.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- QUOTE -->
  <section class="tao-quote" aria-label="Opening quote">
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "Plan the hard while it is easy;
        do the great while it is small."
      </p>
      <cite>— Laozi, Tao Te Ching</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE TAO -->
  <section class="tao-section tao-pink">

    <div class="tao-section-heading">
      <p class="tao-number">01 / THE TAO</p>

      <h2>
        STOP<br>
        FORCING<br>
        EVERYTHING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tao-copy">

      <p>
        Taoism encourages you to live in accordance
        with the Tao, or the Way, rather than
        trying to control everything around you.
        Balance doesn't mean eliminating change.
        It means learning to respond to change
        without fighting every part of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        With bipolar disorder, you can't always
        control your mood, energy, or thoughts.
        You can recognize what's happening,
        respect your limitations, and make
        choices that support your stability.
      </p>

      <p class="tao-takeaway">
        ACCEPT WHAT'S HAPPENING.<br>
        RESPOND TO WHAT YOU CAN.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 / YIN AND YANG -->
  <section class="tao-section tao-yellow">

    <div class="tao-section-heading">
      <p class="tao-number">02 / YIN AND YANG</p>

      <h2>
        TWO SIDES.<br>
        ONE<br>
        PERSON.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tao-copy">

      <p>
        Yin and yang represent complementary
        qualities such as darkness and light,
        stillness and movement. They remind
        you that opposing experiences can
        exist within a larger whole.
      </p>

      <p>
        Depression and mania aren't spiritual
        forces that need to balance each other.
        They're medical symptoms. But neither
        extreme defines who you are.
        You aren't more authentic when you're
        manic or less worthy when you're depressed.
      </p>

      <p class="tao-takeaway">
        YOU ARE MORE THAN EITHER EXTREME.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 / WU WEI -->
  <section class="tao-section tao-black">

    <div class="tao-section-heading">
      <p class="tao-number">03 / WU WEI</p>

      <h2>
        DO LESS.<br>
        FORCE<br>
        LESS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tao-copy">

      <p>
        Wu wei means acting without unnecessary
        force. It isn't about doing nothing.
        It's about working with your circumstances
        instead of exhausting yourself
        trying to fight them.
      </p>

      <p>
        During depression, you may need to
        simplify your responsibilities, rest,
        and accept help. During mania, you
        may need to stop acting on every
        urgent idea. Not every thought
        needs to become a purchase,
        project, message, or decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes the most useful thing
        you can do is nothing right now.
      </p>

      <p class="tao-takeaway">
        AN URGE IS NOT AN INSTRUCTION.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 / HUMILITY -->
  <section class="tao-section tao-purple">

    <div class="tao-section-heading">
      <p class="tao-number">04 / HUMILITY</p>

      <h2>
        YOU DON'T<br>
        KNOW<br>
        EVERYTHING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tao-copy">

      <p>
        Mania can make you feel absolutely
        certain about your ideas, decisions,
        and interpretations. But feeling
        convinced doesn't mean you're right.
      </p>

      <p>
        Taoism encourages humility about
        what you know. You can question
        your certainty, listen to people
        you trust, and delay major decisions
        without giving up your independence.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have to distrust every
        thought. You just don't have to
        believe every thought immediately.
      </p>

      <p class="tao-takeaway">
        CERTAINTY IS A FEELING.<br>
        NOT PROOF.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 / SIMPLICITY -->
  <section class="tao-section tao-white">

    <div class="tao-section-heading">
      <p class="tao-number">05 / SIMPLICITY</p>

      <h2>
        NOT<br>
        EVERYTHING<br>
        NEEDS<br>
        ANALYZING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tao-copy">

      <p>
        Living with bipolar disorder can turn
        every mood, thought, and impulse into
        something to examine. Awareness matters,
        but constant self-monitoring can
        become exhausting.
      </p>

      <p>
        Taoism invites you to return to
        ordinary life. Eat, rest, spend
        time with your dog, and focus
        on what's directly in front of you.
        You don't need to understand
        every feeling before continuing
        with your day.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nourishing your life also means
        caring for your body through sleep,
        food, treatment, and routines that
        work with your physical limitations.
      </p>

      <p class="tao-takeaway">
        SOMETIMES LIFE IS JUST LIFE.<br>
        LET IT BE.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 / LIVING WITH BIPOLAR -->
  <section class="tao-section tao-yellow">

    <div class="tao-section-heading">
      <p class="tao-number">06 / LIVING WITH BIPOLAR</p>

      <h2>
        FIND<br>
        YOUR<br>
        OWN WAY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tao-copy">

      <p>
        Taoism doesn't ask you to enjoy
        bipolar disorder or treat it
        as necessary for spiritual growth.
        You don't need mania to appreciate
        creativity or depression to
        understand suffering.
      </p>

      <p>
        Accept your symptoms without
        surrendering to them. Recognize
        your limitations without turning
        them into your identity. Continue
        taking your medication, getting
        support, and making decisions
        that protect your well-being.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can't force your mind into
        perfect stability. Sometimes
        the right response is to act.
        Sometimes it's to rest.
        Sometimes it's to recognize
        that the most urgent feeling
        in the room doesn't deserve
        to be in charge.
      </p>

      <p class="tao-takeaway">
        YOU DON'T HAVE TO CONTROL EVERYTHING<br>
        TO TAKE CARE OF YOURSELF.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- CLOSING -->
  <section class="tao-ending">

    <div>
      <p class="tao-ending-small">CLOSING</p>

      <h2>
        FOLLOW<br>
        THE <span>WAY.</span>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="tao-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Balance isn't about never changing.
        It's about recognizing where you are,
        responding to what's real, and
        knowing when to act and when to
        let things be.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "tao-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>
